==Russian==
{{wikipedia|lang=ru}}
===Etymology===
From {{etyl|sla-pro|ru}} {{term/t|sla-pro|*zolto}} (Old Church Slavonic {{term|злато|tr=zlato|lang=cu}}), from {{etyl|ine-pro|ru}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*ǵʰel-||yellow; gleam; to shine}}, probably through a later root {{term/t|ine-pro|*ǵʰl̥-tó-m||gold}}. Cognate with Ukrainian {{term|золото|tr=zoloto|lang=uk}}, Belarusian {{term|золата|tr=zolata|lang=be}}, Bulgarian {{term|злато|tr=zlato|lang=bg}}, Serbian {{term|злато|tr=zlato|lang=sh|sc=Cyrl}}, Slovene {{term|zlato|lang=sl}}, Czech {{term|zlato|lang=cs}}, Slovak {{term|zlato|lang=sk}}, Polish {{term|złoto|lang=pl}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latvian {{term|zelts|lang=lv}} and Proto-Germanic {{term/t|gem-pro|*gulþą}} (whence English {{term|gold|lang=en}}).

